data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1793626935082,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"Crew: Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module\",\"Service Module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with Nixon, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ended; the program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Uncrewed Soviet probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21, while Armstrong and Aldrin were still on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved in Washington, D.C.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth; Alan Shepard followed on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\",\"Sputnik crisis\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rockets' capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed mission to the Moon was the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade is out.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development and building much larger alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters until the superior one was determined.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed additional funds for other engine development and for unmanned exploration, to ensure the survival of the first man to make the flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Urgent National Needs\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"In a real sense, going to the Moon will be an entire nation's effort, since all must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards are hostile to all mankind.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that the conquest of space deserves the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answered the question of why the Moon with rhetorical comparisons to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Atlantic\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the US chooses to go to the Moon this decade because the challenge is hard and worth accepting and winning.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a 'moondoggle'.\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was already named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing with Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy renewed the joint lunar expedition proposal at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the joint idea was abandoned after his death.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three parts: the command module, service module, and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"Only the command module, which housed the three astronauts, returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"This design allowed a single Saturn V rocket, then under development, to launch the spacecraft.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Project Gemini developed technologies and techniques required for Apollo; NASA also adopted MOSFETs and silicon IC chips.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Saturn V\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"lunar module\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and abruptly halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"Apollo 7 in October 1968 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"Apollo 8 in December 1968 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 9 in March 1969 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit, and Apollo 10 in May 1969 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module\",\"Service Module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Space Race\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Urgent National Needs\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Atlantic\",\"Texas\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"5afea334f1523bf85eae68611b09d94934b91e3210b1fd732a65a75f4f13e88f"}}

